2c07e105-d815-4088-b8ff-55e863cd14e0	805735ef-0d55-462d-99fd-f15056c0e530	assistant	To find the airport code with the highest number of flights, we need to consider both the source and destination airports for each flight. We can achieve this by querying the `flights` table and counting the occurrences of each airport code.\n\n```sql\nSELECT SourceAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\nFROM flights\nGROUP BY SourceAirport\nUNION ALL\nSELECT DestAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\nFROM flights\nGROUP BY DestAirport\nORDER BY FlightCount DESC\nLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nHowever, the above query may not give accurate results if there are multiple airports with the same highest number of flights. A more accurate approach would be to use a subquery to calculate the total flight count for each airport and then select the airport with the maximum count.\n\n```sql\nSELECT AirportCode\nFROM (\n  SELECT SourceAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\n  FROM flights\n  GROUP BY SourceAirport\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT DestAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\n  FROM flights\n  GROUP BY DestAirport\n) AS AirportFlights\nGROUP BY AirportCode\nORDER BY SUM(FlightCount) DESC\nLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nThis query first calculates the total flight count for each airport by considering both source and destination airports. It then selects the airport code with the highest total flight count. \n\nHowever, if there are multiple airports with the same maximum flight count, this query will return only one of them. If you want to get all airports with the maximum flight count, you can use the following query:\n\n```sql\nWITH AirportFlights AS (\n  SELECT SourceAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\n  FROM flights\n  GROUP BY SourceAirport\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT DestAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\n  FROM flights\n  GROUP BY DestAirport\n),\nRankedAirports AS (\n  SELECT AirportCode, SUM(FlightCount) AS TotalFlightCount,\n         RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(FlightCount) DESC) AS Rank\n  FROM AirportFlights\n  GROUP BY AirportCode\n)\nSELECT AirportCode\nFROM RankedAirports\nWHERE Rank = 1;\n```	SELECT SourceAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\nFROM flights\nGROUP BY SourceAirport\nUNION ALL\nSELECT DestAirport AS AirportCode, COUNT(*) AS FlightCount\nFROM flights\nGROUP BY DestAirport\nORDER BY FlightCount DESC\nLIMIT 1;	2026-05-01 10:21:01.470651+07	chat	null
