36ca5ec7-02b0-49f5-b873-6513a0ccf0b0	e9ec69a1-4aef-42f8-81c6-db14960f8c30	assistant	To find the airport with the least flights, we need to count the number of flights for each airport and then select the one with the minimum count. We can achieve this by using SQL queries that involve counting and grouping.\n\n```sql\nSELECT T1.AirportCode, T1.AirportName\nFROM airports AS T1\nINNER JOIN (\n  SELECT \n    SourceAirport AS AirportCode, \n    COUNT(*) AS FlightCount \n  FROM flights \n  GROUP BY SourceAirport\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT \n    DestAirport AS AirportCode, \n    COUNT(*) AS FlightCount \n  FROM flights \n  GROUP BY DestAirport\n) AS T2\nON T1.AirportCode = T2.AirportCode\nORDER BY T2.FlightCount ASC\nLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nThis query works as follows:\n\n1. It first counts the number of flights for each airport as a source and destination separately.\n2. It then joins these counts with the airports table to get the airport names.\n3. Finally, it orders the results by the flight count in ascending order and limits the output to the airport with the least flights.\n\nHowever, the above query might not work correctly if there are airports with the same least number of flights. A more accurate approach would be to use a subquery or a Common Table Expression (CTE) to first calculate the total flights for each airport and then find the minimum.\n\nHere's a more accurate version:\n\n```sql\nWITH AirportFlightCounts AS (\n  SELECT \n    AirportCode, \n    COUNT(*) AS TotalFlights \n  FROM (\n    SELECT SourceAirport AS AirportCode FROM flights\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT DestAirport FROM flights\n  ) AS AllFlights\n  GROUP BY AirportCode\n)\nSELECT T1.AirportCode, T1.AirportName\nFROM airports AS T1\nINNER JOIN AirportFlightCounts AS T2\nON T1.AirportCode = T2.AirportCode\nWHERE T2.TotalFlights = (SELECT MIN(TotalFlights) FROM AirportFlightCounts);\n```\n\nThis version correctly handles cases where multiple airports have the same minimum number of flights.	SELECT T1.Airport