9ca162ad-7c70-401f-984f-bc80e18fc9c8	65fea489-86b7-4f9f-99da-2ff276176a44	assistant	The provided SQL query seems to be designed to retrieve store performance metrics, including total successful orders, total revenue, average order value, top-selling item, and the quantity of the top-selling item. However, there are a few issues with the query:\n\n1. The `stores` table is not provided in the schema.\n2. Some subqueries are not optimized and may lead to performance issues.\n\nAssuming that the `stores` table has a column named `store_name` and `id`, here's a revised version of the query:\n\n```sql\nWITH \n    store_performance AS (\n        SELECT \n            o.store_id,\n            COUNT(o.id) AS total_successful_orders,\n            ROUND(SUM(o.total_after_discount)::numeric, 0) AS total_revenue,\n            ROUND((SUM(o.total_after_discount) / COUNT(o.id))::numeric, 0) AS avg_order_value\n        FROM \n            orders o\n        WHERE \n            o.order_status = 'Đã giao'\n        GROUP BY \n            o.store_id\n    ),\n    \n    top_selling_items AS (\n        SELECT \n            o.store_id,\n            f.title,\n            SUM(od.quantity) AS total_quantity,\n            ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY o.store_id ORDER BY SUM(od.quantity) DESC) AS row_num\n        FROM \n            orders o\n        JOIN \n            order_detail od ON o.id = od.order_id\n        JOIN \n            food f ON od.food_id = f.id\n        WHERE \n            o.order_status = 'Đã giao'\n        GROUP BY \n            o.store_id, f.id, f.title\n    )\n\nSELECT \n    s.store_name,\n    sp.total_successful_orders,\n    sp.total_revenue,\n    sp.avg_order_value,\n    tsi.title AS top_selling_item,\n    tsi.total_quantity AS top_item_quantity\nFROM \n    stores s\nJOIN \n    store_performance sp ON s.id = sp.store_id\nJOIN \n    top_selling_items tsi ON s.id = tsi.store_id AND tsi.row_num = 1\nORDER BY \n    sp.total_revenue DESC;\n```\n\nThis revised query uses Common Table Expressions (CTEs) to improve readability and performance. It also uses window functions to efficiently retrieve the top-selling item for each store. Note that the `stores` table schema is assumed to have `store_name` and `id` columns. Adjust the query according to your actual schema.	WITH \n    store_performance AS (\n        SELECT \n            o.store_id,\n            COUNT(o.id) AS total_successful_orders,\n            ROUND(SUM(o.total_after_discount)::numeric, 0) AS total_revenue,\n            ROUND((SUM(o.total_after_discount) / COUNT(o.id))::numeric, 0) AS avg_order_value\n        FROM \n            orders o\n        WHERE \n            o.order_status = 'Đã giao'\n        GROUP BY \n            o.store_id\n    ),\n    \n    top_selling_items AS (\n        SELECT \n            o.store_id,\n            f.title,\n            SUM(od.quantity) AS total_quantity,\n            ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY o.store_id ORDER BY SUM(od.quantity) DESC) AS row_num\n        FROM \n            orders o\n        JOIN \n            order_detail od ON o.id = od.order_id\n        JOIN \n            food f ON od.food_id = f.id\n        WHERE \n            o.order_status = 'Đã giao'\n        GROUP BY \n            o.store_id, f.id, f.title\n    )\n\nSELECT \n    s.store_name,\n    sp.total_successful_orders,\n    sp.total_revenue,\n    sp.avg_order_value,\n    tsi.title AS top_selling_item,\n    tsi.total_quantity AS top_item_quantity\nFROM \n    stores s\nJOIN \n    store_performance sp ON s.id = sp.store_id\nJOIN \n    top_selling_items tsi ON s.id = tsi.store_id AND tsi.row_num = 1\nORDER BY \n    sp.total_revenue DESC;	2026-06-18 19:44:58.635343+07	chat	null
